1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,140
In today's lecture, I'm going to talk about Monosodium Glutamate

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:08,400
or MSG, as it's more commonly known.

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:11,368
Now, MSG as you probably know,

4
00:00:11,368 --> 00:00:17,800
is a flavour enhancer which is used particularly in Chinese and Japanese cooking.

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:24,310
Today I am going to explore why it is so popular in these cuisines and,

6
00:00:24,310 --> 00:00:28,650
more importantly, how does it enhance the flavour of food?

7
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:36,090
The main reason why MSG is more commonly used in Japanese meals is tradition.

8
00:00:36,090 --> 00:00:43,715
For many thousands of years the Japanese have incorporated a type of seaweed known as kombu in their cooking,

9
00:00:43,715 --> 00:00:48,530
as they discovered it had the ability to make food taste better.

10
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:53,126
But it wasn't until 1908 that the ingredient in kombu

11
00:00:53,126 --> 00:01:01,400
which was responsible for the improvement in flavour was actually discovered to be glutamate by scientists working there.

12
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:09,653
From 1908 until 1956, glutamate was produced commercially in Japan by a very slow

13
00:01:09,653 --> 00:01:12,600
and expensive means of extraction.

14
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,938
It was in 1956 that the speed of the process was improved,

15
00:01:17,938 --> 00:01:21,240
and industrial production increased dramatically

16
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:23,720
and still continues to increase to this day.

17
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:30,690
In fact, hundreds of thousands of tonnes of MSG are produced all over the world today.

18
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:34,800
So what exactly is MSG?

19
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:40,104
Well, Monosodium Glutamate contains seventy-eight point two per cent glutamate,

20
00:01:40,104 --> 00:01:47,000
twelve point two per cent sodium and nine point six per cent water.

21
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,953
Glutamate is an amino acid that can be found naturally in all protein-containing foods,

22
00:01:53,953 --> 00:01:58,920
erm, so this includes food such as meat and cheese.

23
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:02,718
It is widely known that Chinese and Japanese food contains MSG

24
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:09,970
but many people don't seem to be aware that it is also used in foods in other parts of the world.

25
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:14,634
For example it is found in commercially made Italian pizzas,

26
00:02:14,634 --> 00:02:21,630
in American fast food and in Britain MSG is used in things like potato crisps.

27
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:25,230
So, how exactly does MSG work?

28
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:30,210
Well, in the Western world, we commonly talk of four 'tastes',

29
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:37,000
and I'm sure you're all familiar with the concepts of sweet, sour, bitter and salt.

30
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,350
Well, in 1908, Kikunae Ikeda identified a fifth 'taste'.

31
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:50,470
And it is thought that MSG intensifies this naturally occurring 'taste' in some food.

32
00:02:50,470 --> 00:02:57,361
It does make perfect evolutionary sense that we should have the ability to detect or taste glutamate

33
00:02:57,361 --> 00:03:02,630
because it is the amino acid which is most common in natural foods.

34
00:03:02,630 --> 00:03:07,322
John Prescott, an associate professor at the University of Chicago,

35
00:03:07,322 --> 00:03:13,890
suggests that this fifth taste serves a purpose just as the other tastes do.

36
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:19,645
He suggests that it signals to us the presence of protein in food,

37
00:03:19,645 --> 00:03:25,400
in the same way that sweetness indicates that a food contains energy-giving carbohydrates.

38
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:29,996
Bitterness, he says, alerts us of toxins in the food,

39
00:03:29,996 --> 00:03:35,970
while sourness warns us of spoilage and saltiness signals the presence of minerals.

40
00:03:35,970 --> 00:03:40,550
So, what else do we know about this fifth taste...
